{"about":"da6f74bed07c54a4f0de22cdecdce71da65119ad40ce1aa00214ae8c81c9aeca","body":"The conjecture relies on the assumption that a theory which has survived severe tests is the 'most likely to reveal its falsehood quickly' if it is indeed false. However, this logic is circular: if a theory has already survived the severest possible tests available, there is no logical basis within Popper's framework to claim it is now *more* susceptible to immediate falsification than a theory that has not been tested at all. In fact, a theory that has withstood severe tests has demonstrated resilience, making the claim that it is the optimal candidate for imminent collapse counter-intuitive and unsupported by the definition of corroboration itself.\n[would settle: Evidence showing that historically, theories with high corroboration are falsified faster than theories with low or zero corroboration when subjected to further testing.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}